&#8220;I&#8217;m here to conquer who I am as an artist. In these side quests — these cool things I like to do — I never lose sight of the main goal. I’m still trying to do every stadium in the United States and the world,&#8221; Scott said, shedding light into his mindset as he heads into his next record, which will be his first since 2023&#8217;s Utopia . &#8220;I’m working on an album right now, and that’s f&#8211;king amazing. And I’m constantly pushing myself to push the sonics to try to make something that I haven’t made before.&#8221;

As far as his musical influences at the moment, La Flame revealed he&#8217;s been listening to a mix of Parliament, Oasis, Van Halen, Don Toliver, Raphael Saadiq and Daft Punk.

&#8220;I need to double down on trying to make something that can reach the person that’s all the way back in the bowl, so how do I reach them like the person that’s right next to me,&#8221; he continued. &#8220;So that’s what I’m on — trying to elevate the show to a more theater aspect. Stay tuned on how that’s going to happen. Utopia was supposed to be a play. I want to try to bring back true experiences before they try to take them all down and make everything digital.&#8221;

The 35-year-old&#8217;s Utopia topped the Billboard 200 in 2023 with 496,000 total album-equivalent units earned in the first week, according to Luminate. The project was nominated for best rap album at the 2024 Grammy Awards, but lost to Killer Mike&#8217;s Michael .

Scott has long been open about his goals to capture a Grammy, which has eluded him thus far after 10 nominations. Perhaps an Oscar could come first, with the Houston native teaming up with James Blake and Ludwig Göransson for &#8220;When I&#8217;m Home,&#8221; which landed on The Odyssey soundtrack and plays during the film&#8217;s ending credits.

“I still need a Grammy, but I would love to take home an Oscar. That’d be cool,&#8221; he told Variety . &#8220;I like to set goals, and I just want to achieve them. I noticed the people that get them always have that like, &#8216;It doesn’t matter.&#8217; To me, the award represents something, and to achieve that would be amazing.&#8221;

On the Academy Award quest, Scott discussed his company Cactus Jack signing a first-look deal with Paramount Pictures. &#8220;I want to bring back amazing films, pictures and just creative good ideas,&#8221; the rapper told the publication. &#8220;And [Paramount’s] not scared to make good ideas.&#8221;

The first look deal with Paramount will allow La Flame to develop projects in film, television, theater and live-entertainment spaces.

&#8220;Creativity emanates from every pore of his body,” Paramount Pictures co-chair Josh Greenstein, who Variety reports bonded with Scott over their appreciation for The Warriors , told the magazine. &#8220;From the way he dresses to the cars to the music to his taste in film.&#8221;

